{"archetypes":[{"name":"monitor-shell-bridge","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The breadcrumb delivery mechanism depends on breadcrumb-monitor.sh driving the Monitor primitive from inside a Bash subprocess, but Monitor is a Claude Code tool invocation pattern rather than a shell command or line-print protocol.","prompt_body":"The plan describes `scripts/breadcrumb-monitor.sh` issuing a Monitor command 'via a tiny printed `monitor:start path=<stream>` line if Monitor is invocable from Bash' but defers this as 'implementation detail per the test plan' — investigate whether any confirmed mechanism in the repo allows a shell subprocess to trigger the harness Monitor tool, or whether this capability is undocumented and may not exist. If Monitor is unavailable from Bash, the `wc -c`-offset fallback becomes the sole delivery path; audit the plan's specification of that fallback for completeness: polling cadence, write-visibility race conditions between the `printf` writer in `lib-quiet.sh` and the offset-bookmark reader, and the 'DONE sentinel observed → loop exits within 1 second' timing claim under macOS filesystem semantics. Check that the fallback's Bash 3.2 portability is confirmed by `scripts/test-breadcrumb-monitor.sh`'s cases, particularly for `wc -c` offset bookkeeping and `trap EXIT` composition with `larch_quiet_install_done_sentinel`. Confirm that `scripts/breadcrumb-monitor.md` (listed in the file set but not yet created) fully specifies which path is primary versus fallback and under what observable conditions each activates, since the test plan's 'stream file grows mid-loop → emitted lines reach stdout near-instantly' claim is unverifiable without knowing which path is active. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"lint-migration-scope","focus_area":"risk-integration","weight":1,"rationale":"The lint repurposing changes mandatory banner and per-anchor comment phrases currently embedded across a large Markdown surface; the plan must enumerate all sites and confirm the rewritten lint actively rejects old phrases rather than only accepting new ones.","prompt_body":"The plan redefines `scripts/lint-foreground-markers.sh`'s required phrases — old: `**⚠ Foreground required — do NOT set \\`run_in_background: true\\`.**` and `# Foreground required: see BASH_AUTHORING.md §4`; new: `**⚠ Background required — must be paired with breadcrumb-monitor.sh.**` and `# Background pair required: see BASH_AUTHORING.md §4` — and asserts the old phrasing 'is no longer accepted in tracked Markdown; CI fails on it,' but the rewrite target list names only ~10 files while the old foreground-marker pattern likely appears in many more `skills/*/references/` and `.claude/` files not enumerated. Grep `skills/`, `.claude/`, and `docs/` for the literal old banner phrase and old per-anchor comment string to establish the full existing surface, then compare against the files the plan explicitly lists as rewrite targets to identify gaps. Verify that `scripts/test-lint-foreground-markers.sh`'s new test cases explicitly exercise that old-phrase patterns produce a non-zero exit and a diagnostic naming the migration, not merely that new-phrase patterns pass lint. Check whether `.pre-commit-hooks.yaml`, any CI workflow YAML, or `docs/linting.md` embed the old banner string as a literal example, since those files would fail the new lint but are not in the plan's rewrite surface. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"env-signal-coherence","focus_area":"architecture","weight":1,"rationale":"The foreground-duplication guard uses LARCH_QUIET_BREADCRUMBS_ALREADY_SURFACED as a cross-process signal between sibling processes, but a child process cannot modify a sibling's environment, making the described guard mechanically impossible as written.","prompt_body":"The plan sets `LARCH_QUIET_BREADCRUMBS_ALREADY_SURFACED=1` in the parent before launch so both the background script and `breadcrumb-monitor.sh` inherit it as siblings, then describes `lib-quiet.sh` 'unsetting it inside the child when FD-3 is actually wired to a real terminal/harness stream' — but a child process's `unset` cannot propagate to a sibling process's environment, so `breadcrumb-monitor.sh` will always observe the var as set (1) and will always exit silently, silencing every breadcrumb for every launch including the background case the feature exists to serve. Determine whether the intended cross-process signal requires a sentinel file side-channel (parallel to the `LARCH_DONE_SENTINEL` file already in the plan) rather than an env var mutation, and whether the test case 'foreground-duplication guard: when `LARCH_QUIET_BREADCRUMBS_ALREADY_SURFACED=1`, the helper prints nothing to chat and exits cleanly' inadvertently validates the broken behavior rather than the correct one. Also check whether the four new env vars (`LARCH_BREADCRUMB_STREAM`, `LARCH_BC_DEPTH`, `LARCH_DONE_SENTINEL`, `LARCH_QUIET_BREADCRUMBS_ALREADY_SURFACED`) have fully specified reset and inheritance semantics in `scripts/lib-quiet.md` for the nested-`larch_quiet_init` case, and whether the `LARCH_QUIET_DISABLE=1` regression assertion in `test-lib-quiet.sh` is confirmed to cover the new emission target path and not only the legacy FD-3 path. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
